data.content:
Here's your test rewritten with proper MSTest 3.x+ collection assertions:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetActiveUsers_ReturnsOnlyActiveUsers()
{
    var service = new UserService();
    var users = service.GetActiveUsers();

    Assert.IsNotEmpty(users);
    Assert.IsTrue(users.All(u => u.IsActive));
    Assert.HasCount(3, users);
    var admin = Assert.ContainsSingle(users.Where(u => u.Role == "Admin"));
}
```

**What changed:**

| Original | Replacement | Why |
|----------|-------------|-----|
| `Assert.IsTrue(users.Count > 0)` | `Assert.IsNotEmpty(users)` | Clearer failure message |
| `Assert.IsTrue(users.Count() == 3)` | `Assert.HasCount(3, users)` | Shows expected vs actual count on failure |
| `Single()` + `Assert.IsTrue(admin != null)` | `Assert.ContainsSingle(...)` | Asserts exactly one match and returns it |

The `users.All(u => u.IsActive)` check doesn't have a direct single-assertion equivalent, so `Assert.IsTrue` is still appropriate there. Alternatively, you could loop and assert each item if you want per-element failure messages.
